 \chapter*{Abstract}
 \addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Abstract}
-\initial{C}ommunity detection in graphs is an \textsc{np}-hard problem, which is relevant not only for large-scale real-world graphs but also for its many applications. The problem imposes a trade-off between the topological quality of the resulting partitions and computational scalability. Deep neural models offer great expressive power, but at the cost of prohibitive memory consumption. This cost is worsened by rising hardware prices, driven by the computational demand for AI. Classical modularity-based heuristics, on the other hand, scale well but ignore node features.
+\initial{C}ommunity detection in graphs is an \textsc{np}-hard problem, which is relevant not only for large-scale real-world graphs but also for its many applications. The problem imposes a trade-off between the topological quality of the resulting partitions and computational scalability. Deep neural models integrate topology and node features, but most of them require the number of communities $k$ to be fixed in advance. Classical modularity-based heuristics, on the other hand, scale well but ignore node features.
 
-This thesis proposes a single-layer \textit{Graph Convolutional Network} (\textsc{gcn}) that optimizes the Soft Modularity $Q'$ in an unsupervised manner. The architecture employs the Leiden algorithm as a structural prior to initialize centroids and attenuate spatial noise during message passing. A dynamic filter selects the partitions with the highest internal cohesion in order to anchor the latent vectors before maximizing $Q'$ via gradient ascent.
+This thesis proposes a single-layer \textit{Graph Convolutional Network} (\textsc{gcn}) that optimizes the Soft Modularity $Q'$ in an unsupervised manner. The architecture employs the Leiden algorithm as a structural prior to define the community centroids. A dynamic filter keeps the largest communities, which anchor the latent vectors before maximizing $Q'$ via gradient ascent.
 
-This work evaluates the model with four metrics (\textsc{nmi}, \textsc{ari}, Modularity $Q$, and \textsc{dbi}). The data are nine real-world graphs from four domains (up to 34{,}493 nodes) and synthetic Stochastic Block Model graphs (up to 10{,}000 nodes). Performance is compared against Louvain, Leiden, DeepWalk, \textsc{dgi}, DMoN, and the base GCN-Louvain architecture, with five seeds per configuration. \textsc{ram} and \textsc{vram} consumption is quantified to test the central hypothesis. A single-layer architecture, guided by a high-quality modular prior, matches deep models in topological quality at a fraction of their memory footprint.
+This work evaluates the model with four metrics (\textsc{nmi}, \textsc{ari}, Modularity $Q$, and \textsc{dbi}). The data are nine real-world graphs from four domains and synthetic Stochastic Block Model graphs (up to 10{,}000 nodes). The proposal is evaluated on eight of the real-world graphs, since on Coauthor Physics (34{,}493 nodes) the dense modularity matrix exceeds the \textsc{gpu} memory. Performance is compared against Louvain, Leiden, DeepWalk, \textsc{dgi}, DMoN, and the base GCN-Louvain architecture, with five seeds per configuration. A single-layer architecture, guided by a modular prior, yields partitions of competitive quality with respect to deep models, although it does not outperform them, without receiving $k$ as input and with greater stability across seeds than \textsc{dgi}.
